    <div id="LifetimeRAPM" class="dataset-tabcontent" style="display: none;">
        <h3 class="rapm-title">28 Year Lifetime RAPM: Measuring Career Impact Relative to Age</h3>

        <div class="expandable-content" id="lifetime-rapm-info">
            <div class="content-preview">
                <span class="rapm-subtitle">This is the most important table on the site</span>
            </div>
            <div class="content-full" style="display: none;">
                <span class="rapm-subtitle">
                    <h4>What is it?</h4>
                    <p>28-Year Lifetime RAPM is a stat that measures how much a player impacts winning over their entire career, relative to their age, using data from 1997 to 2024.</p>

                    <h4>Key points:</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>It looks at how well a team performs when a player is on the court vs. off the court.</li>
                        <li>It accounts for the quality of teammates and opponents.</li>
                        <li>It uses an "age curve" to estimate a player's value at different ages.</li>
                        <li>It measures how well a player performs relative to expectations for their age.</li>
                        <li>Regularized Adjusted Four Factor data is also available for a player's career.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h4>Four Factors and Their Importance:</h4>
                    <p>The Four Factors are key components that contribute to a team's success. They are:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>EFG (Effective Field Goal Percentage):</strong> Measures shooting efficiency, accounting for the fact that 3-pointers are worth more than 2-pointers.</li>
                        <li><strong>TOV (Turnover Percentage):</strong> The rate at which a team turns the ball over per possession.</li>
                        <li><strong>ORB (Offensive Rebound Percentage):</strong> The rate at which a team rebounds its own missed shots.</li>
                        <li><strong>FTR (Free Throw Rate):</strong> The rate at which a team gets to the free throw line relative to its field goal attempts.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <p>These factors are calculated using on/off court data. By comparing how these factors change when a player is on the court versus off, we can estimate their career impact in each area on both ends of the court.</p>

                    <p>Importantly, a player's Four Factors can predict their non-age adjusted RAPM with a 0.95 r^2. This means the Four Factors are extremely valuable in explaining why a player may have a high or low RAPM.</p>

                    <h4>Why use an age curve?</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>It helps the model be more accurate over long time periods.</li>
                        <li>Traditional RAPM calculations value a player as a constant, which isn't realistic over a long career.</li>
                        <li>The age curve allows the model to account for typical improvement and decline patterns.</li>
                        <li>It helps compare players fairly across different stages of their careers.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h4>What's special about it?</h4>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Long-term view: It considers a player's entire career, not just individual seasons.</li>
                        <li>Age-relative performance: It measures how well a player performs compared to expectations for their age.</li>
                        <li>Four Factors analysis: It breaks down a player's impact into specific areas, helping explain their overall RAPM.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h4>Things to keep in mind:</h4>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Not perfect: Some players age differently than the typical curve, which can affect their rating.</li>
                        <li>Limited data: For older players, we're missing data from before 1997.</li>
                        <li>Team effects: A player's rating can be influenced by long-time teammates.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h4>How to use it:</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Use it as one tool among many to evaluate a player's career impact.</li>
                        <li>Look at the Four Factors to understand why a player rates high or low in RAPM.</li>
                        <li>Consider how a player performed relative to expectations for their age throughout their career.</li>
                        <li>Remember that it's a career estimate, not an exact measure of a player's peak value.</li>
                        <li>A player's peak 4 or 5 year rapm is a better estimate of their peak in the NBA. Some guys just decline way faster than others.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <p>By considering both the overall RAPM and the Four Factors, you can get a more complete picture of how a player contributes to their team's success over their career, taking into account their performance relative to their age and specific areas of impact.</p>
                </span>
            </div>
            <button class="expand-button">More Info</button>
        </div>

